// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: modules, name-resolution, arity, silent-failure
// @expected-error: E100
// @expected-error: E100
// @expected-error-count: 2
// @description: A call through a module path is resolved and counted,
//   like every other call.
//
// It was neither. A call whose receiver is a module path of THREE or
// more segments accepted any leaf name, accepted any middle segment,
// produced a fresh type variable that satisfied whatever the caller
// declared, and reached the runtime as a stub. And the argument COUNT
// was not checked at any path length: `a.b.g()` against
// `fn g(x: Int) -> Int` checked clean, ran, and answered 1 — the
// missing argument read as an unset register.
//
// The neighbourhood was all checked, which is why this survived so
// long: a two-segment path reports E100 for a missing leaf, a missing
// method on a VALUE reports E400, a missing field reports E404, and a
// three-segment chain rooted in a value reports E404 too. Only the
// module-path form was exempt, and the exemption was invisible because
// its symptom is a call that does nothing rather than a call that fails.
//
// Two mechanisms, both now closed:
//
//   * The arity comparison in the global-registry arm was a FILTER. A
//     candidate with the right name and the wrong count was skipped, the
//     arm answered "not a module call", and the caller fell through to
//     leniency — so a real callee invoked wrongly was indistinguishable
//     from a name that does not exist. A name that resolves and a count
//     that does not is now reported as an arity error.
//
//   * The inline-module arms carried a ±1 tolerance "for self-param
//     counting", borrowed from method resolution. A module's free
//     function has no `self` to be unsure about and Verum has no default
//     parameters, so the count is exact.
//
// And resolution now asks the question it was avoiding: when the path
// names a module we know about and the member is not in it, that is an
// unbound name. `a.b.absent` and `a.zzz.f` are the two errors this spec
// pins — the missing LEAF and the missing MIDDLE.
//
// The positive controls live in `module_path_call_still_resolves.vr`,
// which must keep checking clean: a fix that rejected working
// module-path calls would satisfy this file and break the language.

module a {
    public module b {
        public fn f() -> Bool {
            true
        }
    }
    public fn top() -> Bool {
        true
    }
}

fn main() {
    // Missing LEAF: `a.b` is a module, `absent` is not in it.
    let _leaf = a.b.absent();

    // Missing MIDDLE: `a` is a module, `zzz` is not in it.
    let _middle = a.zzz.f();

    print("unreachable");
}
